def add(title,scans,lines,kind,body,context,register,queries,confidence=None,source=None,section='III. Further handwritten scope tests and derived formalism'):
    i=f'N{len(ITEMS)+1:02d}'
    ITEMS.append(dict(id=i,title=title,scans=scans,lines=lines,kind=kind,body=body,context=context,register=register,queries=queries,confidence=confidence,source=source,section=section))
    return i

add('A degree-zero obstruction to unrestricted nonproper analytic comparison','006','20–30','own idea',r'''Take $X=\mathbb A^1_{\mathbb C}$, $A=\mathbb Z/n$ with $n>1$, and $F=\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb Z}i_{m*}A$, with $i_m$ the integer-point inclusion. Quasi-compactness on the algebraic side gives $H^0_{\mathrm{ét}}(X,F)=\bigoplus_m A$, whereas analytic sheafification permits arbitrary values on the closed discrete integer set, giving $H^0(\mathbb C,F^{\mathrm{an}})=\prod_m A$. Thus the comparison is a non-surjective inclusion in degree zero, testing the phrase “pour $\forall F$ faisceau de torsion” after deletion of properness. The verified published comparator imposes properness or constructibility, so the calculation does not challenge that qualified statement.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N18–N19, scans112/120: adds a different nonproper obstruction; the earlier boundary-support projection example is not re-derived. No matching analytic direct-sum/product example occurs in any supplied file.','README.md, S19; MPL_33_098-146 §76. No same-point settled result.',['analytic','direct sum','comparison','nonproper','support'],'High: both section groups and the comparison map are explicit. The claim concerns the unrestricted literal scope, not an unstated historical convention.','Artin (1973c), XVI, Theorem 4.1; The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Lemma 59.51.4, Tag 03Q5, for algebraic filtered-colimit cohomology.')
add('A proper smooth example with nonfree cohomology over a fixed ring','010','2','own idea',r'''Let $E/\mathbb C$ be an elliptic curve with a connected étale double cover, $A=\mathbb Z/4$, and $F$ the rank-one $A$ local system whose deck involution acts by $-1$. Then $H^0(E,F)=A^{(-1)}=2A\simeq\mathbb Z/2$, which is not free over $A$. Consequently “sont loc. libres” fails if it means preservation of freeness over that fixed ring, while local constancy on the point is unaffected. This distinguishes the literal module condition from the proper smooth local-constancy theorem.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N25, scans126–132: adds a geometric sign-local-system example to the earlier perfect-complex/nonprojective-cohomology lead; its two-term complex is cited, not recomputed. N19, scans112/120, has a related scope distinction.','MPL_33_098-146 N25 and N23; no status upgrade.',['nonprojective','locally free','locally constant','N25'],'High for the invariant-module calculation; conditional on the fixed-coefficient meaning of “libre”.','Artin (1973b), XV, Theorem 2.1; Artin (1973c), XVI, Theorem 2.1.')
add('The rough base-change sketches do not determine a theorem by themselves','003','3–54','reading question',r'''The rough page places $X,X',Y,Y',k,k',K$ in several diagrams, with uncertain labels such as “$g(U')$” and “$\supset U'\ne\varnothing$.” It is plausible to compare the drawings with the neighboring geometric-point and field-extension discussion, but the uncertain shafts and labels do not specify a unique argument or assertion. No missing arrowhead, commutativity claim, or theorem has therefore been supplied from that context.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N04 and N07, scans104–106: thematic base-change parallels only; no matching sketch or label string was found. Adds a reading task rather than a new base-change result.','README.md/S19 and MPL_33_098-146 §76; no LM4/LM7 inference.',['base change','geometric','g(U','diagram','field'])
add('Flat pullback is exact, so the injective-resolution wording is harmless','038','18–48','unpursued implication',r'''The phrase “définition directe via résolutions injectives” belongs to a “morphisme plat de topos annelés,” where $f^*$ is exact and an injective replacement is unnecessary but harmless. The nonflat paragraph deliberately changes to “résolutions plates” on $D^-$ and makes preservation of $D^+$ and $D^b$ conditional on finite Tor dimension. The modern all-$D$ construction uses K-flat resolutions; it extends the framework without turning the earlier flat-case sentence into an error.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N02–N06, scans100–106: restates and precedes the flat/nonflat distinction; N08/N10, scans108/112, already record the finite-ring infinite-Tor issue, which is not re-derived here.','MPL_33_098-146 N08, §76, §78.',['flat','injective','Tor','unbounded','resolutions'],source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), §21.18, Tag 06YV.')
add('The resolution in derived Hom needs an explicit admissibility condition','040','2–14','unproved claim',r'''The definition $R\mathrm{Hom}(F^\bullet,G^\bullet)=\mathrm{Hom}^*(F^\bullet,C^\bullet(G^\bullet))$ and its internal-Hom analogue require a specified resolution and totalization convention. A bounded-below injective resolution provides one familiar admissible setting, while arbitrary complexes require a suitable K-injective replacement rather than merely injective terms. The boxed global/internal identity is a derived comparison, not permission to omit these hypotheses.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N02/N08, scans100 and108/112, and N07, scan106: adds a derived-Hom resolution obligation to their pullback/tensor discussion; no identical C• resolution construction is provided.','MPL_33_098-146 N08, §76.',['RHom','injective','resolution','internal','unbounded'])
add('Degree-zero Hom is separated from the whole derived Hom object','040','16–27','unpursued implication',r'''The sheet explicitly labels $\mathrm{Hom}(F^\bullet,G^\bullet)$ as $\mathrm{Ext}^0$ and records shifts for higher Ext. This provides a useful internal control when comparing later passages that identify a morphism group with a whole $R\Gamma R\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}$ object: the group is obtained in degree zero. It does not retrospectively insert a missing $H^0$ into the later or current transcription.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N34, scan144: adds an earlier explicit degree-zero convention to that note’s type discrepancy; N39, scan146, concerns a different factor mismatch.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; N34 is a supplied first-pass lead, not a separately registered settled theorem.',['degree-zero','H^0','Ext','Hom','N34'])
add('The two Ext spectral-sequence sketches retain convergence and index questions','040','29–38','reading question',r'''The first display has the unresolved superscript $1\mid q$ on the Ext sheaf, while the second is annotated “pris terme à terme.” Interpreting them as local-to-global Ext and a termwise hyper-Ext construction requires specifying boundedness or another convergence mechanism and how the bicomplex is totalized. Familiar spectral-sequence formulas cannot decide the doubtful index, the doubled H alphabet, or the partly unfinished syntax of these sketches.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N05, scan104, and N33, scans138–142: adds separate convergence/index loci; no identical spectral-sequence display was found.','MPL_33_098-146 N08 and §76 at the formalism level.',['spectral','hyper','bounded','indices','convergence'])
add('Quasi-compactness replaces a uniform fiber bound on the sheet','042','23–36','withdrawn in the same text',r'''The condition “$f$ est à fibres de dim bornée” is cancelled and replaced by “$Y$ est quasi compact” in the extension of compactly supported direct image to all $D$. In the compactifiable finite-type setting, a quasi-compact base supplies a uniform geometric bound and hence a bound controlling the displayed $D^-$ and $D^b$ restrictions. Existence on the unbounded category and preservation of globally bounded subcategories remain logically distinct claims.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N09, scan110, and N31–N32, scans138–142: adds an earlier explicit geometric uniformity condition; it differs from the later merely-noetherian expectation and does not prove that expectation.','MPL_33_098-146 N30 and §76; N31/N32 remain leads.',['quasi-compact','uniform','cohomological dimension','noetherian','amplitude'],source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Tag 095U and §63.10, Tag 0G28.')
add('A non-quasi-compact base permits unbounded geometric cohomological degrees','042','23–36','own idea',r'''Let $Y=\coprod_{r\ge0}\operatorname{Spec}k$, $X=\coprod_{r\ge0}\mathbb P^r_k$, and $f:X\to Y$ be componentwise proper, with $k$ algebraically closed and $A=\mathbb Z/n$ prime to its characteristic. The constant sheaf $A_X$ lies in degree zero, but $R^{2r}f_*A$ is nonzero on the $r$th component, so $Rf_!A=Rf_*A$ is not globally bounded above. This is a geometric test of the uniform bound needed to preserve $D^-$ or $D^b$, rather than an obstruction to defining a functor on all $D$.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N31, scans138–140: adds a geometric direct-image example to its pointwise-versus-uniform amplitude lead; the earlier shifted-point complex is not re-derived. N32, scans140–142, has the bounded positive comparator.','MPL_33_098-146 N30; no contradiction with the qualified later bound.',['disjoint','uniform','amplitude','bounded','projective'],'High: each component has nonzero top projective-space cohomology, and properness is local on this disjoint base.','The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Tags 095U and 0G28, for the qualified bound; this example is our calculation.')
add('Proper derived base change has an unbounded modern extension','044','2–4,15–27','unpursued implication',r'''The constant coefficient ring makes the étale inverse-image functors exact, and “Lorsque $f$ est propre ce est un Isom” states the derived comparison on $D^+$. A modern proper-base-change theorem applies to all complexes of $\mathbb Z/n$-modules, even without requiring $n$ invertible. This extends the bounded formulation here and says nothing comparable for arbitrary nonproper ordinary direct image.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N04/N07, scans104–106: adds the proper étale comparison; differs from N18, scan120, which concerns failure of an ordinary nonproper projection formula.','MPL_33_098-146 N01, §76, §78; no open-entry status changes.',['proper','base change','unbounded','D^+','nonproper'],source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Lemma 59.92.3, Tag 0F0C.')
add('The base-change passage changes from a complex to a sheaf','044','15–27','reading question',r'''The comparison is introduced for $F^\bullet\in D^+(X)$, but the next formula “Passant aux faisceaux de coh.” uses $F$ and $R^if_*F$. For a general complex the cohomology object is $\mathcal H^i(Rf_*F^\bullet)$, so recovering the claimed consequence requires either specializing to a sheaf in degree zero or a bounded-below hypercohomology argument. The literal $g^+$, the deleted reversed arrow, and the retained forward arrow remain documentary features rather than notation to normalize.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N33, scans138–142, and N34, scan144: adds a complex/sheaf type transition to their notation checks; no exact formula settles it.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; no new theorem claimed.',['hypercohomology','complex','cohomology sheaves','base change','notation'])
add('Finite-ring Poincaré duality does not demand free cohomology','046,048','046:2; 048:2–10','unpursued implication',r'''The title-only scan046 contains no omitted proof, while scan048 gives $H^i_!(X,F)^\vee\simeq H^{2d-i}(X,\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(F,\mu_n^{\otimes d}))$ for locally constant coefficients in the smooth setting. Duality into $A=\mathbb Z/n$ is exact because $A$ is self-injective, so finite local systems need not be free for this reduction from derived Hom to underived duality. Perfectness requirements for endomorphism traces are a separate issue.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N08, scans108/112, N23/N25, scans126–132, and N29, scans134–136: adds a distinction between exact duality into this ring and finite Tor/projectivity; it does not contradict their finite-ring caveats or assert that every finite module is perfect.','MPL_33_098-146 N08, N23, N25, N29.',['duality','nonprojective','finite coefficient','injective','trace'],source='Deligne (1973), SGA 4 XVIII, §§3.2.5–3.2.6.')
add('The relative and partial-range duality assertions need their precise antecedents','048','12–22','unproved claim',r'''The relative passage assumes local constancy of $R^if_!F$, then proposes a degreewise duality description, and finally says “la conclusion” persists in the range $j\ge2d-i_0$. The immediately preceding conclusion is the doubtful insertion “constructiblt.|constructibilité,” making constructibility a plausible antecedent rather than local constancy or the entire degreewise isomorphism. A reconstruction must fix this antecedent, coefficient finiteness, and the smooth/compactifiable hypotheses inherited from the preceding title before claiming a range theorem.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N19, scans112/120, and N09, scan110: adds another local-constancy/boundedness scope question; no matching partial-range theorem appears.','MPL_33_098-146 N30 and §76; no previous lead supplies the missing theorem.',['locally constant','constructib','range','duality','cohomological dimension'])
add('A boundary test excludes the stronger partial-range interpretation only','048','22','own idea',r'''Set $S=\mathbb A^1_{\mathbb C}$, $X=\mathbb A^2_{\mathbb C}\setminus\{0\}$, $f(x,t)=t$, and $F=A=\mathbb Z/n$, $n>1$; every fiber is a connected nonproper curve, so $R^if_!A=0$ for $i\le0$. Localization at the removed codimension-two point, followed by affine-line invariance, gives $i_{0*}A(-2)[-4]\to A_S\to Rf_*A\to$, hence $R^3f_*A\simeq i_{0*}A(-2)$. This constructible skyscraper is not locally constant, and after the displayed twist it remains nonconstant, so low-range local constancy cannot imply the stronger local-constancy or degreewise-duality assertion for every $j\ge2$. It does not refute the plausible constructibility antecedent of N49.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N19, scans112/120, and N09, scan110: adds a new smooth open-boundary test; it does not repeat N18’s projection-formula example or establish failure of the stated constructibility consequence.','MPL_33_098-146 N30, §76; no status change.',['boundary','locally constant','constructib','projection','duality'],'High for the localization calculation; applicability is expressly conditional on the stronger reading of “la conclusion”.','Artin (1973b), XV, Corollary 2.2; Artin (1973c), XVI, Theorem 3.7, for the two inputs.')
add('The relative-duality formulas retain base-sheaf and support inconsistencies','048','14–20','reading question',r'''The coefficient target is written $(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)_X$ even though $R^if_!F$ is an object on the base $S$. Moreover the explanatory line retains $R^{-i}_!f_*(DF)$ while the neighboring displays cancel the compact-support exclamation. These are source/target and support questions for collation; the research interpretation uses ordinary direct image only where explicitly stated and never silently repairs the frozen formulas.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N33, scans138–142, and N39, scan146: adds different literal index/type anomalies; N18, scan120, explains why the support distinction is substantive, without deciding these marks.','MPL_33_098-146 N30, §76.',['support','literal','mismatched','subscript','ordinary'])

