add('The repeated n°391 is not a secure seminar-exposé identification','005–037 odd except009; 049','typed headers; 015:6–34','reading question',r'''Seventeen reused leaves bear “n° 391,” predominantly with root-system material and one graded-algebra continuation, but the primary record of Séminaire Bourbaki exposé391 identifies Grisvard’s differential-equation talk, a different subject. The Montpellier catalogue’s cote33 note mentioning that exposé therefore cannot date or identify these particular leaves by their number alone; it may concern other leaves in the cote or a different numbering stream. Bourbaki’s root-system chapter is a credible comparison destination, while exact draft identity and chronology remain unverified.''',TCTX,TREG,['391','Bourbaki','reused','root','bibliograph'],source='Université de Montpellier (n.d.), archive catalogue, cote33 note31; Grisvard (1971); Bourbaki (2007/1968), Chapter VI metadata. No full draft-to-book collation.',section=T)
add('The Coxeter contraction definition is internally incompatible','005','12–16','own idea',r'''The first clause $m'_{hk}=m_{hk}$ for $h\ne i$ or $k\ne i$ overlaps the later clause $m'_{ik}=m_{ik}+m_{jk}$, so the complete literal definition already assigns conflicting off-diagonal values. If the latter clause is evaluated as the intended overriding assignment, an $A_3$ chain $i-j-k$ gives $m'_{ik}=2+3=5$, hence $q'_{ik}=-(1+\sqrt5)/4$, whereas $q_{ik}+q_{jk}=-1/2$. Thus the equality used in the proof fails under that reading; neither replacing “ou” nor inserting a correction into the sum is authorized in the transcription. This is a collation and proof-dependency issue, not an unconditional refutation of the intended finite-Coxeter theorem.''',TCTX+' The searches contain no Coxeter-contraction point; the relation is documentary only.',TREG,['Coxeter','contraction','q_','root','finite'],'High for the conflicting clauses and the conditional A3 calculation; the intended convention is unknown.',source='Own exact calculation; records/reused_root_formula_checks.json. Bourbaki (2007/1968), Chapter VI is only a comparison target.',section=T)
add('The finite-Coxeter classification distinguishes necessity from realization','007,019,021','007:5–21; 019:8–24; 021:24–65','unproved claim',r'''The phrases “il restera ensuite à voir” and “Il restera naturellement à vérifier” distinguish graph restrictions from realization by reduced irreducible root systems. The Pythagorean estimate on007 proves several necessary constraints, whereas the lattice method on019 additionally requires spanning and integral reflection pairings before the classification list on021 can be realized. Reconstruction should keep the missing surrounding lemmas and type-by-type realization steps separate from the defective contraction formula in N53.''',TCTX+' No corresponding classification problem is recorded; this adds the actual proof architecture of these foreign leaves.',TREG,['Coxeter','root','classification','réduit','irreducible'],source='Frozen source and its explicit cross-references; Bourbaki (2007/1968), Chapter VI for future collation.',section=T)
add('The lowest allowed A-rank is an exception to the endpoint pairing','013','5–19','own idea',r'''The heading permits $\ell=1$, but the endpoint assertion $(\widetilde\alpha\mid\alpha_1)=(\widetilde\alpha\mid\alpha_\ell)=1$ then identifies both endpoints with the same root. In the displayed coordinates $\widetilde\alpha=\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$, so the pairing is $2$. The value $1$ is correct for the two distinct endpoints when $\ell\ge2$, making the rank-one exception a precise source-level scope check.''',TCTX+' No A1 or highest-root exception appears in the context; this adds an independent boundary case.',TREG,['A_','highest root','rank','root system'],'High: it is the squared norm of the explicit vector on the same page.',section=T)
add('The root-table roadmap says sum where the tables give half-sum','015,023,037','015:23; 023:6–13; 037:29–35','reading question',r'''Item(VII) in the roadmap says “La somme des racines positives,” while the later corresponding entries explicitly compute “La demi-somme” and denote it by $\rho$. The difference is a factor of two in the described invariant, not a harmless change of label. The later coordinate calculations may help collate the roadmap, but its literal wording remains untouched.''',TCTX+' The context’s N33, scans138–142, is only a methodological parallel for incompatible source labels; no same root-table item exists.',TREG,['demi','rho','root','table','notation'],section=T)
add('The E6 exponent equation is arithmetically inconsistent as printed','023','29–43','own idea',r'''The text lists exponents $1,5,7,11$ and two integers $m,m'$ with $m+m'=12$, but the product formula uses $(12+1)$ in the last known factor. Its stated Weyl-group order gives $(m+1)(m'+1)=51840/(2\cdot6\cdot8\cdot13)=540/13$, impossible for integers. Replacing only that factor by $(11+1)$ would give $45$ and the pair $\{m,m'\}=\{4,8\}$, a proposed research emendation rather than a change to the source.''',TCTX+' No E6-exponent calculation is supplied; this adds a literal arithmetic test.',TREG,['exponent','E6','Weyl','root','product'],'High: the denominator13 contradicts integrality without any external root-system table.',source='Own exact arithmetic in records/reused_root_formula_checks.json.',section=T)
add('One asymmetric Cartan integer does not determine the length ratio','035','8–15','own idea',r'''For the displayed convention $n(u,v)=2(u\mid v)/(u\mid u)$, choose simple roots $u=(1,-1)$ and $v=(0,1)$ in $B_2$. Then $n(u,v)=-1$, but $\|v\|/\|u\|=1/\sqrt2$ and $s_us_v$ has order4, contrary to the sentence assigning ratio1 and order3 to that single value. The usual determination uses both Cartan integers, or orders the pair so that $u$ is no longer than $v$; no such ordering is stated in this paragraph.''',TCTX+' No asymmetric Cartan-pairing claim occurs in the context; this adds a distinct scope test.',TREG,['Cartan','B_2','length','root','reflection'],'High: the inner product and two reflections are explicit; a convention on an unseen preceding page could restrict applicability.',source='Own exact matrix calculation in records/reused_root_formula_checks.json.',section=T)
add('Global root-length normalization needs an irreducibility or component convention','033,035','033:16–35; 035:23–33','reading question',r'''The definition begins with a reduced system and a global shortest length, then says its labels are either only1, only1/2, or only1/3. A reducible orthogonal sum can contain both $B_2$ and $G_2$ components normalized to the same short length, producing labels1,2,3 simultaneously, and arbitrarily rescaled components need not fit that global list at all. Recovering an inherited irreducibility or componentwise-normalization convention is therefore necessary before using the one-factor rescaling argument on033 in unrestricted reducible generality.''',TCTX+' No matching normalization convention was found; adds a source-scope question, not a claim that classification of reduced systems fails.',TREG,['irreducible','reduced','normalization','root','Dynkin'],section=T)
add('The E8 highest-root sign conflicts with the displayed dual bases','037,049','037:23–26; 049:7,11–30','own idea',r'''Scan037 identifies $\widetilde\alpha=\omega_8$, and scan049 defines the fundamental weights as the basis dual to the simple coroots. In the equal-length normalization the supplied coordinates recover $\alpha_8=-\varepsilon_6+\varepsilon_7$, so $(\widetilde\alpha\mid\alpha_8)=(\varepsilon_7+\varepsilon_8\mid-\varepsilon_6+\varepsilon_7)=1$. This conflicts with the literal $-1$ in scan049; the contradiction is internal to the displayed normalization and not borrowed from a published correction.''',TCTX+' No E8 weight/pairing calculation appears; this adds a new internal check.',TREG,['E8','weight','highest root','coroot','sign'],'High: the dual-basis property and direct coordinate pairing independently determine the sign under the stated normalization.',source='Own exact rational calculation, records/reused_root_formula_checks.json.',section=T)
add('The E8 ambient dimension and E7 ordering remain collation questions','031,049','031:25–35; 049:7,11','reading question',r'''The E8 paragraph writes $V=R^4$ although its eight fundamental weights span an eight-dimensional coordinate space. Separately, the E7 discussion names an induced lexicographic order, whereas the E8 highest-root sentence specifies the opposite lexicographic order. The dimension is internally incompatible with the displayed basis, while the ordering discrepancy requires recovery of the surrounding convention before deciding whether it is substantive.''',TCTX+' No matching ambient-space or lexicographic convention occurs; adds two closely related coordinate-convention loci for collation.',TREG,['lexicographic','R^4','E7','E8','coordinate'],section=T)
add('The E8 weight and rho tables provide a positive internal consistency check','037,049','037:7–35; 049:11–30','own idea',r'''Treating the eight displayed weights as dual to the simple coroots recovers a norm-squared2 simple-root basis, and every displayed root-coordinate expansion of those weights agrees with it. Their sum has coordinate vector $(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,23)$ and simple-root coefficients $(46,68,91,135,110,84,57,29)$, exactly the $\rho$ row on037. Thus the isolated sign and dimension questions in N59–N60 do not justify distrusting or silently rewriting the entire table.''',TCTX+' No matching weight-table computation was found; adds a positive check rather than re-deriving an earlier registered result.',TREG,['weight','rho','E8','matrix','root'],'High for the stated algebraic identities; this checks the supplied table, not historical source identity.',source='Own exact rational arithmetic in scripts/check_reused_root_formulas.py and its JSON output.',section=T)
add('The graded-algebra page links Tor vanishing to regularity under missing hypotheses','011','6–17','unproved claim',r'''The continuation invokes vanishing of $\operatorname{Tor}_j(k,M)$ above $r$ and regular-local theory to recover an earlier condition(a), whose full statement is not present here. The later Hilbert-series product follows from a homogeneous polynomial-generator description and determines the degree multiset under those hypotheses. The regular-local finite-projective-dimension criterion is a precise modern comparator, but neither it nor the visible product licenses extending the assertion to arbitrary graded rings.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N08/N10, scans108/112, and N25, scans126–132: adds a regular-ring finite-dimension contrast without re-deriving their nonregular finite-ring examples. notes/RESULTS_STATE.md, WG2 §2.1 #2: differs from its graded Lefschetz projectors; mere grading is not a substantive match.','MPL_33_098-146 N08, N25, §76; WG2 §2.1 #2 remains unchecked.',['graded','Tor','regular','Hilbert','Lefschetz'],source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Proposition 10.110.5, Tag 00OC.',section=T)
add('The hyperalgebra uncertainty is an editorial decision in a reused source','009','9,15–19','self-flagged: a step the source itself marks as unverified',r'''“On ne sait si l’on définira en général la notion d’hyperalgèbre” asks whether a definition will be included in the planned exposition. Together with “doit être fait” and “On pourrait donner les sorites suivants,” it records editorial placement and proposed additions, not a conjecture that hyperalgebras cannot be defined. The “Tribu n°62” label and foreign typed state must remain separate from the handwritten cohomology author’s own research questions.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N55, scan145, and §65: adds another reused-source editorial/verification record; no hyperalgebra or Tribu62 match appears.','MPL_33_098-146 §65 and §1; no implication for LM7_PUSH C.6 follows from shared algebraic vocabulary.',['hyperalg','Tribu','editorial','formal group','small'],section=T)
add('The topological-dual formal-group dictionary needs the finiteness convention','009','5–7,21–31','unproved claim',r'''The equivalence between augmented completed-algebra maps and coalgebra multiplication, together with the weak-topology and square-zero formulas, depends on which duals and completed tensor products are meant. Cartier’s primary construction gives a useful comparison under a filtration-compatible free-basis hypothesis. That comparison identifies a hypothesis to recover from the missing pages, rather than identifying this “Tribu” sheet as Cartier’s text or proving its full generality.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md: no same hyperalgebra/completed-dual construction occurs; N42, scan099, is a different analytic category, and N56 records unrelated source candidates. Adds a separate comparison branch.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; no direct bearing on LM7 E.23 or LM7_PUSH C.6.',['hyperalg','coalgebra','completed','formal','duality'],source='Cartier (1955–1956), §2; verified comparator, not source identification.',section=T)
add('The bilinear bracket and convolution sentence retain source-level gaps','009','11–13','reading question',r'''The source gives $[x,y]=B(x,y)-B(y,x)$ after writing the multiplication “mod. deg.2,” and then says the additive convolution algebra is “$(M)$.” One must recover the filtration convention behind the degree cutoff and collate whether a functor symbol is missing before the final parentheses; the expected divided-power expression from the preceding line cannot be inserted on that expectation alone. The three stated calculation levels—intrinsic, semi-intrinsic, and coordinates—make this a useful notation comparison within the formal-group source.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md, N33 and N39, scans138–142/146: methodological type/notation parallels only; no matching bracket or convolution sentence. Adds a new collation task.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; no class-two arithmetic-section inference.',['bracket','convolution','divided','formal','notation'],source='Cartier (1955–1956) is background only; no exact textual match was established.',section=T)
add('The formal-points exact sequence deliberately limits surjectivity','009','35–39','unproved claim',r'''The final arrow of $1\to G_J\to G_A\to G_{A/J}\dashrightarrow1$ is expressly qualified by “slt si $\dim G$ finie ou $J$ ouvert.” Recovering the topology on $A$, the meaning of $G_J$, and the missing proposition4 is necessary before proving the lifting assertion. The dashed arrow and its qualification should therefore remain part of any mathematical reconstruction rather than becoming unconditional right exactness.''','same_cote_33/MPL_33_098-146_notes.md: no exact formal-points lifting or closed-ideal sequence was found; N55, scan145, gives only a different missing-source-hypotheses parallel. Adds a new formal-group proof obligation.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; none of the LM4/LM7 section-map rows concerns this formal-group sequence.',['formal','closed ideal','surject','G_A','lifting'],section=T)

