add('A two-point counterexample to the product-stalk Hom assertion','041,043','041:17–26; 043:10–23','own idea',r'''On the Sierpiński space with opens $\varnothing,\{\eta\},X$, an abelian sheaf is an arrow $A\to B$, and the typescript’s $P_X$ sends it to $A\oplus B\xrightarrow{\mathrm{pr}_B}B$. A morphism between such $P_X$ objects has matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\0&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ on $X$ and $d$ on $\{\eta\}$, leaving an extra term $b\in\operatorname{Hom}(B,A')$ absent from the claimed coordinatewise product. For the constant $\mathbb Z$ sheaf, $(m,n)\mapsto(m+n,n)$ and $n\mapsto n$ give that extra morphism explicitly. Thus “It is easily checked that these maps are inverses” fails even if the free global index $U$ is read as $X$ for purposes of testing the claim.''',UC+', N54, scan145: methodological parallel only, testing another reused-source assertion; no product-stalk or Sierpiński formula occurs. Adds a distinct counterexample.','MPL_33_098-146 N54 methodologically; '+UR,['stalk','Sierp','P_X','product','skyscraper'],'High: all sheaves, restrictions, and the off-diagonal map are explicit in abelian groups.',source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Tags 009B–009C; the counterexample is our calculation.',section=U)
add('The correct adjunction has a different first stalk object','041,043','041:17–26; 043:10–34','own idea',r'''For abelian sheaves the stalk–skyscraper adjunction gives $\operatorname{Hom}(P_XF,P_XF')\simeq\prod_x\operatorname{Hom}((P_XF)_x,F'_x)$. The first stalk is generally $(P_XF)_x$, not $F_x$; in N66 it is $A\oplus B$ at the closed point rather than $A$. This explains the false fullness assertion while leaving the printed faithfulness argument, which uses the monomorphism $\rho_{F'}$, available for separate evaluation.''',UC+': no matching skyscraper-adjunction point; §76 maps other derived operations on scans100–146. Adds a different categorical correction in the research apparatus.',UR,['skyscraper','adjunction','stalk','fullness','faithful'],'High: the formula follows from the product universal property and the verified adjunction.',source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Lemma 6.27.3, Tag 009C.',section=U)
add('The sheafification formulas have independent source and index mismatches','041,043','041:15,20–21; 043:12–22','reading question',r'''Scan041 changes $x$ to $x'$ in one naturality line and invokes a projection over $V$ while quantifying points of $U$. Scan043 writes global sheaf Hom with an unbound open $U$ on the right and uses $(\rho_{F'})_x$ in a proposed composite declared to start at $F_x$. These type/index questions require collation, but repairing them alone would not remove the off-diagonal morphism in N66.''',UC+', N39, scan146, and N46, scans105/109/111/131/135/139/143: adds new mismatched-factor/index loci; none supplies the present formulas.',UR,['mismatched','index','rho','Hom','type'],section=U)
add('Exact sheafification depends on the missing categorical definitions','043,045','043:36–41; 045:3–20','unproved claim',r'''“If $\underline A$ is right perfect then $R_X$ is exact” follows a reflection statement requiring completeness and enough small objects, with definitions I,4.1 and I,5.2 unavailable here. The visible proof uses exactness of the directed colimit defining a stalk and only the left exactness of products actually written. Kleisli and Wu retain an additional small-object hypothesis in their related category-valued argument; the modern abelian-sheaf case supplies a baseline, not a proof of the unidentified source’s entire generality.''',UC+': no same right-perfect/small-object or sheafification definition was found; adds a hypothesis-recovery task to §76 rather than restating its derived-category comparisons.',UR,['right perfect','small objects','sheafification','exact','reflection'],source='Kleisli and Wu (1964), p.422; The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), §18.3, Tags 03CM and 03CO.',section=U)
add('The reflection can be proved without the false product-Hom formula','039','22–30','own idea',r'''Assume relevant subsheaves of $P_XF$ form a set, sheaves are closed under the needed limits, and every sheaf unit $G\to P_XG$ is monic. For $\varphi:F\to G$ to a sheaf, the pullback $H=P_XF\times_{P_XG}G$ is then a subsheaf of $P_XF$ through which $\rho_F$ factors, so minimality of the intersection $R(F)$ supplies $R(F)\to G$. The equalizer of two such factorizations is again a subsheaf containing the image of $F$, and minimality proves uniqueness. This conditional proof isolates size and monicity from the invalid claim that every $P_X$ morphism is coordinatewise.''',UC+': no matching intersection-reflection construction or proof occurs; adds an independent proof route to the comparison programme, with no earlier scan settling its premises.',UR,['intersection','reflection','subsheaf','equalizer','minimality'],'High for the conditional proof; the unseen Chapter I must establish its premises for the source’s coefficient category.',source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), §19.11, Tag 05AB, Lemma 19.11.1, supplies one sufficient set-of-subobjects hypothesis.',section=U)
add('Enough injectives has its own proof dependencies','039','7–12','unproved claim',r'''The conclusion “has enough injectives” needs the relevant abelian, filtered-colimit, and generator properties of the sheaf category, not the invalid fullness assertion. A stalkwise injective construction additionally needs stalk tests to detect monomorphisms, a point distinguished by Kleisli and Wu for category-valued sheaves. Neither the failure in N66 nor the bare word “generator” settles the conclusion in the source’s unidentified general category.''',UC+': no same category-valued enough-injectives theorem occurs; adds a dependency check, distinct from N06’s injective-resolution issue on scan106.',UR,['injectives','generator','monomorphism','category-valued','stalk'],source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Theorem 19.11.7, Tag 079H; Kleisli and Wu (1964), pp.420–422.',section=U)
add('Gray’s Columbia mimeograph is a specific but unconfirmed source lead','039,041,043,045','English typescript throughout','own idea',r'''Kleisli and Wu’s bibliography names J. W. Gray’s Columbia mimeograph on category-valued sheaves, and their discussion uses the same unusual perfection/small-object combination. That makes the mimeograph a useful target for matching wording, numbering, and proofs against these four leaves. No exact textual collation was obtained, so the lead establishes neither authorship of these sheets nor their date.''',UC+', N42, scan099, and N56, scans101–121/125–143: adds a new bibliographic candidate while restating their separation of reused-source evidence from attribution; no Gray/Kleisli/Wu match appears.','MPL_33_098-146 §1 and §76.',['Gray','Kleisli','Wu','mimeograph','sheaf'],'Low for source identity: the reference is verified, but a matching copy was not inspected.',source='Kleisli and Wu (1964), p.423, reference5; no Gray source is presented as inspected.',section=U)
add('An unresolved separation question survives on the cancelled typescript','047','3–5','self-flagged: a step the source itself marks as unverified',r'''“Nous ignorons s’il en est encore ainsi (sans hypothèse de séparation) pour $n$ quelconque” concerns an implication b)$\Rightarrow$c), while the same continuation denies its converse without separation even for $n=1$. The definitions of b) and c) are absent, so neither implication can safely be reconstructed from the general subject of regular sequences. The page-level cancellation and reused state do not establish that the handwritten author adopted this open question or mathematically retracted every assertion on the sheet.''',UC+', N55 and standing index S14, scan145: adds another incomplete reused-source question; no same section19.6 or b)/c) condition appears.','MPL_33_098-146 §65 and §1; no settled result bears directly.',['séparation','separation','19.6','regular sequence','b)'],section=U)
add('Induced and enlarged filtrations differ in a one-variable example','047','10–31','own idea',r'''Use $A=k[t]$, $N=A$, $R=tA$, $J=(t)$, and the original filtration $N_0=A$, $N_k=0$ for $k\ge1$. The source’s definitions give $N'_k=t^kA$, $R'_k=t^kA$, and $R''_k=t^{k+1}A$ for $k\ge1$. With $R''$ the map on associated graded induced by $R\hookrightarrow N$ is zero in every degree, whereas the induced filtration $R'$ gives the exact graded row printed on the sheet. This verifies the substantive warning “en général distinctes” rather than collapsing the two filtrations.''',UC+': no matching induced-filtration calculation; N50–N51, scan135, concern different completeness/regular-local issues. Adds a new example.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; no registered example is re-derived.',['filtration','Artin','graded','R\u0027\u0027','regular sequence'],'High: every submodule and quotient is explicit.',source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), Lemma 12.19.12, Tag 05SP, for exactness with induced/quotient filtrations.',section=U)
add('The filtration discrepancy is measured by Tor under a flatness hypothesis','047','18–35','own idea',r'''For the original filtration concentrated in degree zero, an exact sequence $0\to R\to N\to M\to0$ with $N$ flat gives $(R\cap J^kN)/J^kR\simeq\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(A/J^k,M)$. This is the kernel term in the tensor long exact sequence and identifies the obstruction to equality of $R'_k$ and $R''_k$. If $A$ is Noetherian and $N$ finite, Artin–Rees yields cofinality of the induced and $J$-adic filtrations, but not degreewise equality, as N73 shows.''',UC+', N44, scans109/111 and126–136: adds a kernel-level Tor obstruction to a different proposed finite-resolution/multiplicity link; no Artin–Rees or strictness match was found.','MPL_33_098-146 §76; N25/N40 concern finite complexes and do not settle this filtration issue.',['Tor','filtration','Artin-Rees','strictness','cofinal'],'High under the displayed flatness and finiteness hypotheses; the first equality is an explicit long-exact-sequence application.',source='The Stacks Project Authors (n.d.), §10.75, Tag 00LY; Lemma 10.51.2, Tag 00IN.',section=U)
add('A nonseparated summand can be invisible to an associated graded module','047','3–5','own idea',r'''As a mechanism rather than an identification of the missing b)/c) conditions, take $A=k[t]$, $D=k[t,t^{-1}]/k[t]$, and $M=A\oplus D$. Since $tD=D$, one has $\operatorname{gr}_{(t)}M\simeq k[T]$ and the natural map $(M/tM)[T]\to\operatorname{gr}_{(t)}M$ is an isomorphism, although multiplication by $t$ kills the nonzero class of $t^{-1}$ in $D$. The invisible part is $\bigcap_{n\ge0}t^nM=D$, showing why graded information may fail to detect torsion without separation. This does not decide the source’s unknown implication.''',UC+', N50–N51, scan135: methodological missing-hypothesis parallels only; no same nonseparation example is supplied. Adds a separate test.','MPL_33_098-146 §76 and §65; no new historical or novelty claim.',['nonsepar','separation','graded','regular sequence','divisible'],'High for the elementary module calculation; correspondence with b)/c) remains unresolved.',section=U)
add('The cancelled regular-sequence leaf has a precise comparison target','047','2–8,20,33','reading question',r'''The source header “IV–1007,” section19.6, and formula numbers19.6.1–19.6.2 point to a precise EGA IV comparison target. The primary record for the fourth part was verified, but its large facsimile could not be fully read through the retrieval service, so no word-for-word identity is asserted. The heading’s deleted “quotient” and inserted “filtré,” as well as the whole-page cancellation, remain facts of this source leaf rather than corrections from a later edition.''',UC+', N43–N46, scans101–121 and related source loci: adds another separately paginated reused source; T07–T08 distinguish such pagination from package totals. No identical section is in the context.','MPL_33_098-146 §1 and §76.',['19.6','EGA','1007','filtré','pagination'],source='Grothendieck (1967), EGA IV, fourth part: verified bibliographic metadata only; exact collation remains open.',section=U)

# Final QA refinements. Research prose only; frozen source is never opened for writing.
edit(18,confidence='High for the conditional example: distinct pole orders prime to p prove independence; the primary Artin–Schreier input was verified separately.')
edit(27,body=ITEMS[26]['body']+' The rational-point convention needs recovery from the edited wording “pt sur $k$”; N87 tests the stronger interpretation allowing every closed point.')
edit(26,body=r'''“Alors la suite spectrale implique” uses vanishing below $2d$ for the displayed coefficient sheaf $F=\mu_{nX}^{\otimes d}$ to identify $H^{2d}_Y(X,F)$ with global sections of $\underline H^{2d}_Y(F)$, yielding a supported global class. The mechanism is the degree-$2d$ edge map of the local-to-global support spectral sequence, whose other total-degree-$2d$ terms vanish. Any further passage to a larger support family still needs the support inclusion specified, and the separate degree-two Kummer NB is treated in N86.''')
edit(42,body=ITEMS[41]['body']+' The parentheses $(i)$ here express complex translation through the Ext relation, whereas $(d)$ in the geometric orientation passages denotes a Tate twist.')
edit(48,body=r'''Scan048 gives $H^i_!(X,F)^\vee\simeq H^{2d-i}(X,\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(F,\mu_n^{\otimes d}))$ for locally constant coefficients in the inherited smooth setting. For finite local systems, duality into $A=\mathbb Z/n$ is exact because $A$ is self-injective, so those modules need not be free for this reduction from derived Hom to underived duality. Perfectness requirements for endomorphism traces are a separate issue; the positive comparison used here does retain constructibility.''')
edit(45,body=ITEMS[44]['body'].replace('$A=\\mathbb Z/n$ prime to its characteristic','$A=\\mathbb Z/n$ with $n>1$ prime to its characteristic'))
edit(51,title='The relative-duality formulas place the coefficient sheaf on the wrong named space',body=r'''The coefficient target is written $(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)_X$ even though $R^if_!F$ is an object on the base $S$. Furthermore the Hom on the left must be interpreted sheafwise to match the higher-direct-image sheaf on the right. These are source/target and internal-Hom convention questions; neither subscript nor underlining has been supplied to the frozen text.''')
edit(54,body=r'''On007, “il restera ensuite à voir” defers realization by finite Coxeter groups, whereas on019 “Il restera naturellement à vérifier” concerns reducedness and irreducibility of the lattice root systems. The Pythagorean estimate on007 proves necessary graph constraints, while the separate lattice construction on019 requires spanning and integral reflection pairings before realizing the root-system list on021. Reconstruction must keep these two realization problems and their missing surrounding lemmas separate, rather than treating all finite Coxeter types as crystallographic root systems or relying on the defective contraction formula in N53.''')
edit(59,kind='own idea',confidence='High for the direct-sum scope test; an inherited irreducibility restriction would exclude the example, and the single-rescaling step under matching global labels is not refuted.',body=r'''The definition begins with a reduced system and a global shortest length, then restricts its label set to $\{1\}$, $\{1,2\}$ or $\{1,3\}$. A reducible orthogonal sum can contain both $B_2$ and $G_2$ components normalized to the same short length, producing labels $1,2,3$ simultaneously, and arbitrarily rescaled components need not fit that global list at all. An inherited irreducibility or component-normalization convention therefore needs recovery. If the globally normalized labels do match as stipulated on033, however, one global rescaling does align the Gram matrices; the different corollary issue is N80.''')
edit(61,title='The E8 ambient dimension conflicts with its displayed basis',scans='049',lines='7,11–30',body=r'''The E8 paragraph writes $V=R^4$ although its eight fundamental weights span an eight-dimensional coordinate space. The surrounding reference to $R^8$ and the rank-eight coordinate matrix make this a source-level dimension inconsistency. The transcription retains $R^4$, and a later published ambient dimension would be evidence for collation rather than a new reading of this sheet.''',queries=['R^4','E8','coordinate','dimension','weight'])
